**Vehicle speed (2026-11-12, PR #46) — the old "fuel temperature trim" family IS the vehicle speed chain.** `vehicle_speed` @ 0x40a272 (was `fuel_temperature_trim`, 50+ readers) = 1/256 mph per bit. Source 1: the speed sensor on **TPU-B channel 2** (`cm848_initVehicleSpeedSensorChannel`, period-measurement function 0xD; `cm848_vehicleSpeedSensorInterruptHandler` copies the period and reloads `vehicle_speed_pulse_timeout`); `cm848_calculateVehicleSpeed` = 921600 x 40e6 / (pulses_per_mile x 128 x period) with `vehicle_speed_sensor_pulses_per_mile_cal` = **8000** (EEPROM 0x46e; the Chrysler standard), 3.2 us TPU ticks, 2-150 mph clamps, jump rejection, EMA. Source 2 (J1939 automatic drivetrain): `cm848_calculateVehicleSpeedFromOutputShaft` from the transmission output shaft speed with an axle divisor chosen by switch 31 + [-`two_speed_axle_enable_cal`-]{+`two_speed_axle_switch_present_cal`+} (two-speed axle). The CCVS1 broadcast sends it x 412/256 (mph -> km/h). On this truck it reads 0 (no sensor wired; period still at its 0x5e7fb044 start value). `drivetrain_ratio(_raw)` = engine speed over vehicle / output-shaft speed x 2048. Fault freeze frames store it (`fault_entry_t.vehicle_speed`, was `temperature_trim`). Most other `fuel_temp*` / `fuelTemp*` names were re-domained on 2026-11-13 (e.g. `low_speed_governor_moving_speed_values` / `_axis`, `output_speed_sensor_check_minimum_vehicle_speed`, `low_speed_governor_moving_road_speed`, the `engine_to_vehicle_speed_ratio_*` monitor). Odometer: `vehicle_speed_sensor_pulse_total` deltas go out in PCI transmit message 11 (header 0x5D) with two fuel totals (built by `cm848_buildPciTransmitMessage11DistanceAndFuelUsed`). **The dash odometer itself comes back in on PCI RX[11] header 0x72: `pci_receive_message_11_odometer` @ 0x40b471 (u32 big-endian, 1/8000 mile — 0x8B633FCC = 292,317.2 mi in the RAM dump), so a CLIP 0x4A read of 4 bytes at 0x40B471 returns the cluster's odometer.** It has the same 8000-per-mile unit as `lifetime_distance_pulses`; the ECM only tests it for zero (fault bit 345 in `cm848_runImmobilizerTask`).
